// core:jobs — a general task system and the worker pool the runtime parallelises
// itself with. A job is the C-classic pair — Dado has no closures — of a
// function reference plus a data pointer. The surface is
// stable across two engines — a **serial** one (zero worker threads: every job
// runs inline on submit) and a **pooled** one (a central MPMC queue behind a
// mutex+condvar, N worker threads, batch completion via atomic counters). A
// caller writes the same code either way; threads are only how fast it goes.
//
// **A job hands its result back through a channel.** It has no return value and
// no handle to hang one on, so it `#send`s into a queue channel the caller
// declared, and the caller drains it — once the batch is joined, or on the
// next frame, whichever it wants:
//
//     #channel(64) tile_done(i32 tile, i64 checksum)
//     private i32 g_tiles_lost
//
//     void render_tile(^jobs.JobArgs a):
//         for t in a.begin..<a.end:
//             try #send(tile_done, t, checksum_of(t)) else:
//                 _ = #atomic_fetch_add(&g_tiles_lost, 1, #RELAXED)
//
//     void frame(^jobs.Scheduler s):
//         jobs.parallel_for(s, 64, 4, render_tile, nil)
//         for tile, checksum in #drain(tile_done):
//             show(tile, checksum)
//
// A value is copied into the channel's slot, so nothing the job wrote has to
// outlive it; a `ref` moves to the thread that drains. **Size the channel for
// the batch.** The thread that drains is usually the one waiting, and a wait
// helps by running jobs on the waiting thread — so a job that retried on
// `FULL` there would be waiting on itself. Count the refusal instead, as
// above, or keep the value for a later batch. And **drain from one thread** —
// the one that submits is the natural choice — for the channel's whole life.
//
// So the scheduler's only answer about a job is *finished or not*: `wait` for a
// handle, or `parallel_for`, which joins before it returns. There is no
// polling of a result array through a handle.
//
// Scheduler home is `core:jobs`, not `app:jobs`: no `app`
// dependency, broadly reusable; `app:app` owns one instance and layers the frame
// barrier on top. Engine v1 is the simplest correct one: a
// central queue, tuned for "obviously correct under a sanitiser," not throughput.
//
// **The concurrency contract.** The ambient allocator is per thread, so a job
// allocates the way any Dado code does: a bare `#make`/`#format` in a job
// reads *its worker thread's* `#default`, which is the root — C's
// heap — unless the job opened a `using`, and a `using` a job opens is that
// worker's alone and is undone when its suite ends. **A job runs with its
// worker's ambient, not with its submitter's**: a `using` around `submit` or
// `parallel_for` does not reach a worker, because it changed the ambient on
// the submitting thread only. So the worker's private scratch arena is handed
// over as `JobArgs.scratch`, and the idiom for temporaries is `using
// ja.scratch:` inside the job (or an explicit `#make(T, n, ja.scratch)`) —
// both allocate from an arena no other worker touches, and it is reset with
// `reset_scratch`, so nothing a job means to keep goes there. `core:runtime`'s
// scratch is per thread too, so a job may use `enter_temp`/`close_temp`; it
// is its worker's, and the worker's first scope configures it. A
// `parallel_for` chunk owns a disjoint index range, so nothing here is shared
// across workers; **a map or any other structure a job shares with another
// thread is not synchronised** and needs a `core:threads` mutex.
//
// **Tier W H.** The pooled engine runs wherever `core:threads` does: Linux,
// macOS, the BSDs, Windows, and a web module built with `--web-threads`. A
// single-threaded web module has no thread to start, so there `start` runs
// the serial engine whatever `nthreads` asks for, and answers true;
// `Scheduler.nworkers` says which engine runs. A `#NONE` build is refused by
// the static assertion below: the pool is `core:threads`', and a freestanding
// target has no second thread to run it on.
//
// **The scratch arenas grow on the allocator `start` was called under**
// (`#default`: C's heap, unless a `using` around `start` says otherwise), and
// keep it until `stop`. That is the one allocator this package captures, and
// it is captured on purpose: the arenas outlive the `using` they were made in
// and are reset and grown from every worker thread, whose own ambient is the
// root.
//
// **What raises into a script.** Nothing here. A job is a function reference
// and a `rawptr` to its data, the scheduler's handles are `core:threads`'
// thread and lock handles, and its counters are atomic accesses, so every
// function reaches something a script cannot hold. A script that wants work done in parallel
// asks Dado code that owns a scheduler.
//
// This is a `core:` library and owes tests: it is a concurrency engine and
// wraps core:threads.
//
// ## The atomics are the language's, and the ordering is written at each call
//
// Every atomic access below is a `#atomic_*` intrinsic over an ordinary `i32`
// the scheduler already owns, with its ordering written at the access. The
// verb takes the ordering as a constant, so the C compiler emits exactly that
// instruction; `core:threads`' `atomic_*` functions take an `Order` argument
// and pick the verb with a `switch`, which costs nothing once inlined but
// says less at the call than the verb does. Each ordering here is chosen:
//
//   * **the batch counter's decrement is `#ACQ_REL`** — release, so that a
//     thread which sees the count reach zero sees everything the job wrote
//     before it; acquire, so that the decrementing thread sees the other
//     chunks' decrements. This is the one pairing the whole barrier rests on.
//   * **every read of a batch counter is `#ACQUIRE`**, which is the other half
//     of it: `join_batch` is what observes zero.
//   * **`running` is `#RELEASE` to store and `#ACQUIRE` to read**, the ordinary
//     shutdown-flag pairing.
//   * **`hnext` is `#RELAXED`**, and that is the one worth defending: it is a
//     ticket dispenser and nothing is published through it. Every ticket is
//     distinct because the increment is atomic, and the *slot* it names is then
//     claimed by a compare-exchange that carries its own ordering. A stronger
//     ordering here would buy nothing and cost a fence per submission.
//
// Each `Scheduler` keeps its own workers' identities, so any number of them
// may run at once, each with its own pool.
package jobs

import "core:threads"
import "core:mem"
import "core:mem/arena"

#static_assert(#OS != #NONE, "core:jobs needs core:threads for its pool, and a #NONE target has no second thread")

// The most participants one scheduler has: the calling thread and up to 63
// worker threads. `start` clamps `nthreads` to `MAX_WORKERS - 1`.
const i32 MAX_WORKERS = 64

// The job ring's capacity. A submission into a full ring runs on the
// submitting thread instead.
const i32 QCAP = 8192

// The batch-counter ring's capacity: how many batches can be unfinished at
// once before a submission runs inline.
const i32 HCAP = 8192

// What a job sees. `scratch` is this worker's private arena — `using
// ja.scratch:` or an explicit argument (see the contract above). `begin`/`end` are the sub-range for a
// `parallel_for` chunk; a plain `submit` gets [0, 0).
type JobArgs:
    i32       worker,
    (mem.Proc proc, rawptr data) scratch,
    i32       begin,
    i32       end,
    rawptr    data,

// A job: called with its `JobArgs`, once per `submit` and once per chunk of a
// `parallel_for`. It answers nothing; a result goes out through a channel
// (see the header).
type JobFn: void(^JobArgs)

// A completion token. Serial handles are always done; a pooled handle names a
// batch-counter slot. Block-and-help with `wait`; a job's result comes back
// through a channel (see the header).
distinct type Handle: i32

private const i32 SERIAL_DONE = -1

// `alloc_batch` found no free slot in the whole ring. Not a `Handle` and never
// converted to one: a caller that gets this runs its work inline and answers
// `Handle(SERIAL_DONE)`, which is the *already finished* handle and is correct
// for work that has finished. The two constants share a value because a slot
// index is a non-negative number and both of these are "not one"; nothing reads
// either as the other.
private const i32 NO_BATCH = -1

// One queued unit of work: a chunk of a batch.
private type Job:
    JobFn  fn,
    rawptr data,
    i32    begin,
    i32    end,
    i32    batch,

// A worker's identity, handed to its thread. The scheduler is held as a
// `rawptr` so that `Scheduler` can keep these inline: a `^Scheduler` here
// would make `Scheduler` contain an array of a type that points back at it,
// which C cannot lay out. The worker turns it back with `SchedPtr`.
private type WCtx:
    rawptr sched,
    i32    index,

private type WCtxPtr: ^WCtx

private type SchedPtr: ^Scheduler

// The scheduler, owned by the caller: a package variable, since the inline
// rings make it too big for the stack. Functions take `^Scheduler`. Its zero
// value is stopped; `start` starts it.
type Scheduler:
    threads.Mutex mtx,
    threads.Cond  cv,
    i32    nworkers, // worker THREADS (0 = serial engine)
    i32    running,  // atomic flag: 1 running, 0 = draining to shutdown
    // job ring, guarded by mtx
    [QCAP]Job q,
    i32       qhead,
    i32       qtail,
    i32       qcount,
    // batch counters, atomic; a ring indexed by hnext
    [HCAP]i32 counters,
    i32       hnext,
    // workers 0..nworkers (0 is the main thread, which helps during wait)
    [MAX_WORKERS]threads.Thread wthreads,
    [MAX_WORKERS]arena.Arena warenas,
    bool                     started,
    // each worker's identity, which its thread reads from here
    [MAX_WORKERS]WCtx        wctx,

// ── lifecycle ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Starts the scheduler with `nthreads` worker threads (0 is the serial
// engine, every job inline). Worker indices run 1..nthreads; index 0 is the
// main thread, which runs jobs while it waits. Per-participant scratch arenas
// 0..nthreads are created here, on `#default` (see the header). Answers false
// if a thread or primitive could not be created, having stopped whatever it
// did start. A single-threaded web module starts no thread, so there it
// starts the serial engine and answers true; so does any web build that
// cannot start the first worker.
bool start(^Scheduler s, i32 nthreads):
    i32 nt = nthreads

    if nt < 0:
        nt = 0

    if nt > MAX_WORKERS - 1:
        nt = MAX_WORKERS - 1

    s.nworkers = nt
    s.qhead = 0
    s.qtail = 0
    s.qcount = 0
    s.hnext = 0
    s.running = 1

    // arenas for participants 0..nt
    for i in 0..=nt:
        s.warenas[i] = arena.over(#default)

    if nt == 0:
        s.started = true
        return true

    s.mtx = threads.mutex_new()
    s.cv = threads.cond_new()

    if s.mtx == nil || s.cv == nil:
        threads.mutex_free(s.mtx)
        threads.cond_free(s.cv)
        for i in 0..=nt:
            arena.destroy(&s.warenas[i])
        return false

    // `try_spawn`, not `spawn`: a single-threaded web module answers nil here
    // at run time, and the scheduler falls back to the serial engine, where a
    // `spawn` would refuse the whole build (ERR1033).
    for i in 1..=nt:
        s.wctx[i] = (rawptr(s), i)
        s.wthreads[i] = threads.try_spawn(worker_main, rawptr(&s.wctx[i]))

        if s.wthreads[i] == nil:
            for k in i..=nt:
                arena.destroy(&s.warenas[k])
            s.nworkers = i - 1
            s.started = true

            if i > 1:
                stop(s)
                return false

            // No worker started, so there is no pool to stop: the serial
            // engine is what is left, which the web takes and a native build
            // reports.
            threads.mutex_free(s.mtx)
            threads.cond_free(s.cv)
            s.mtx = nil
            s.cv = nil

            if #OS == #WEB:
                return true

            stop(s)
            return false

    s.started = true
    return true

// Stops the pool: lets the workers drain the queue, wakes and joins them,
// and frees the primitives and arenas. Calling it on a stopped scheduler does
// nothing.
void stop(^Scheduler s):
    if !s.started:
        return

    if s.nworkers > 0:
        threads.lock(s.mtx)
        #atomic_store(&s.running, 0, #RELEASE)
        threads.cond_broadcast(s.cv)
        threads.unlock(s.mtx)

        for i in 1..=s.nworkers:
            if s.wthreads[i] != nil:
                _ = threads.join(s.wthreads[i])

        threads.mutex_free(s.mtx)
        threads.cond_free(s.cv)

    for i in 0..=s.nworkers:
        arena.destroy(&s.warenas[i])

    s.started = false

// Resets every participant's scratch arena, taking back everything jobs
// allocated there; `app:app` calls it once a frame. Call it only while no job
// runs.
void reset_scratch(^Scheduler s):
    for i in 0..=s.nworkers:
        arena.reset(&s.warenas[i])

// ── the surface ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Submits one job. The serial engine runs it now, on this thread; the pooled
// one queues it, and `wait` on the handle returns once it has run.
Handle submit(^Scheduler s, JobFn fn, rawptr data):
    if s.nworkers == 0:
        run_inline(s, fn, data, 0, 0)
        return Handle(SERIAL_DONE)

    i32 batch = alloc_batch(s, 1)

    // Every batch-counter slot is live, so this job has nowhere to record its
    // completion. It runs here instead — `push`'s answer to a full queue, one
    // ring over — and the handle it answers with is the one that is already
    // done, because it is.
    if batch == NO_BATCH:
        run_inline(s, fn, data, 0, 0)
        return Handle(SERIAL_DONE)

    push(s, (fn, data, 0, 0, batch))
    return Handle(batch)

// Submits `fn` to run after `dep` has finished. It waits for `dep` on this
// thread (running other jobs meanwhile) and then submits.
Handle submit_after(^Scheduler s, JobFn fn, rawptr data, Handle dep):
    wait(s, dep)
    return submit(s, fn, data)

// Runs `fn` over [0, n) in chunks of `grain` indices (1 when `grain` is
// below 1), each chunk a job with its range in `JobArgs.begin` and `end`, and
// returns once every chunk has run. It needs no handle: the return is the
// barrier.
void parallel_for(^Scheduler s, i32 n, i32 grain, JobFn fn, rawptr data):
    if n <= 0:
        return

    i32 g = grain

    if g <= 0:
        g = 1

    if s.nworkers == 0:
        chunks_inline(s, n, g, fn, data)
        return

    i32 nch = (n + g - 1) / g
    i32 batch = alloc_batch(s, nch)

    // No slot to count this batch down in, so it is run on this thread — which
    // is also the barrier this function promises, reached the short way.
    if batch == NO_BATCH:
        chunks_inline(s, n, g, fn, data)
        return

    i32 i = 0

    for i < n:
        i32 e = i + g

        if e > n:
            e = n

        push(s, (fn, data, i, e, batch))
        i = e

    join_batch(s, batch)

// Waits for a handle's job to finish, running other queued jobs on this
// thread meanwhile, so a thread at the barrier stays useful.
void wait(^Scheduler s, Handle h):
    if s.nworkers == 0 || i32(h) == SERIAL_DONE:
        return

    join_batch(s, i32(h))

// ── internals ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

private void run_inline(^Scheduler s, JobFn fn, rawptr data, i32 begin, i32 end):
    JobArgs ja = (0, arena.allocator(&s.warenas[0]), begin, end, data)
    fn(&ja)

// Every chunk of [0, n), on this thread. The serial engine's whole loop, and
// also what a pooled engine falls back to when it has no batch counter to spend
// — hoisted so the two are one piece of code rather than two that agree today.
private void chunks_inline(^Scheduler s, i32 n, i32 g, JobFn fn, rawptr data):
    i32 i = 0
    for i < n:
        i32 e = i + g

        if e > n:
            e = n

        run_inline(s, fn, data, i, e)
        i = e

    return

// Run a queued job on participant `idx`, then release its slot in the batch.
private void run_job(^Scheduler s, Job job, i32 idx):
    JobArgs ja = (idx, arena.allocator(&s.warenas[idx]), job.begin, job.end, job.data)
    job.fn(&ja)
    _ = #atomic_fetch_add(&s.counters[job.batch], -1, #ACQ_REL)

// Claim a batch-counter slot and publish `count` into it.
//
// **The store used to be unconditional, on an assumption written in this
// comment**: *the ring is far larger than the in-flight batch count, so a slot
// is reused only long after its batch drained.* That is a hope about arithmetic
// the scheduler does not control. `hnext` wraps every 8192 submissions and
// nothing anywhere holds it back, so a program that submits faster than its
// jobs drain — a producer thread feeding a saturated pool is the ordinary
// way — eventually comes back to a slot whose counter is not zero and
// **overwrites a live batch's count**. Every thread waiting on that batch then
// waits on a number that means something else: `join_batch` returns early and
// its caller reads results from jobs that have not run, or the count never
// reaches zero and the barrier hangs. Neither has a symptom that points here.
//
// So the slot is *claimed* rather than taken. A compare-and-swap against zero
// publishes the count only into a slot whose last batch finished, and a slot
// that is still live is skipped for the next ticket.
//
// **This is the bounded retry loop `tests/cases/atomics.dado` deliberately did
// not write.** That case writes one retry and says why: a bounded loop's
// give-up arm is a line no single-threaded run can reach, and the emitted-C
// coverage audit is right to say so. `jobs` has threads to lose a race to, and
// its give-up arm is reachable — `jobs_test` fills the ring by hand and asks.
//
// The bound is `HCAP` because each attempt takes the *next* ticket, so `HCAP`
// attempts have looked at every slot in the ring; going round again would ask
// the same slots the same question. Answering `NO_BATCH` is not a failure the
// caller has to handle specially: it means the pool is saturated, and every
// caller's response is to run the work inline on the calling thread, which is
// `push`'s existing answer to a full queue and is what makes the ring bounded
// in the first place. It is also the response that makes progress — running a
// job is what frees a slot.
//
// The compare-exchange is `#ACQ_REL` on success, which publishes `count` to
// whoever loads the counter next and acquires whatever the previous occupant
// released; `#ACQUIRE` on failure, where nothing is published and the observed
// value is only being discarded.
private i32 alloc_batch(^Scheduler s, i32 count):
    i32 tries = 0
    for tries < HCAP:
        i32 raw = #atomic_fetch_add(&s.hnext, 1, #RELAXED)
        i32 slot = raw % HCAP

        if slot < 0:
            slot = slot + HCAP

        (i32 _, bool claimed) = #atomic_compare_exchange(&s.counters[slot], 0, count, false, #ACQ_REL, #ACQUIRE)
        if claimed:
            return slot

        tries = tries + 1

    return NO_BATCH

// Enqueue a job, or — if the ring is full — run it inline on the caller to keep
// memory bounded (it still belongs to its batch, so the count stays right).
private void push(^Scheduler s, Job job):
    threads.lock(s.mtx)

    if s.qcount >= QCAP:
        threads.unlock(s.mtx)
        run_job(s, job, 0)
        return

    s.q[s.qtail] = job
    s.qtail = (s.qtail + 1) % QCAP
    s.qcount = s.qcount + 1
    threads.cond_signal(s.cv)
    threads.unlock(s.mtx)

// Pop one job under the lock. Fails when the ring is empty.
private !Job try_pop(^Scheduler s):
    threads.lock(s.mtx)

    if s.qcount == 0:
        threads.unlock(s.mtx)
        fail 1

    Job j = s.q[s.qhead]
    s.qhead = (s.qhead + 1) % QCAP
    s.qcount = s.qcount - 1
    threads.unlock(s.mtx)
    return j

// Help until this batch is drained: run any ready job, else yield (the last
// chunk is finishing on another worker), so the waiting thread gives its time
// slice to the one it waits on rather than spinning on the lock. This is the
// cooperative barrier.
private void join_batch(^Scheduler s, i32 batch):
    for #atomic_load(&s.counters[batch], #ACQUIRE) > 0:
        try Job j = try_pop(s) else:
            threads.yield()
            continue
        run_job(s, j, 0)

// A worker thread: block for work, run it, repeat until drained and shut down.
private rawptr worker_main(rawptr arg):
    WCtxPtr me = WCtxPtr(arg)
    ^Scheduler s = SchedPtr(me.sched)
    i32 idx = me.index

    for:
        threads.lock(s.mtx)

        for s.qcount == 0 && #atomic_load(&s.running, #ACQUIRE) == 1:
            threads.cond_wait(s.cv, s.mtx)

        if s.qcount == 0 && #atomic_load(&s.running, #ACQUIRE) == 0:
            threads.unlock(s.mtx)
            return nil

        Job j = s.q[s.qhead]
        s.qhead = (s.qhead + 1) % QCAP
        s.qcount = s.qcount - 1
        threads.unlock(s.mtx)
        run_job(s, j, idx)

    return nil // unreachable: the loop only exits via the return above
